                "word": "adjoint",
                "pos": "adj",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "75531b3c-3872-3dfe-9d73-0bcd15bf6cb5",
                        "entry_id": "0ab3f277-1430-37ad-9e1d-0e8fff9e73a8",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Used in certain contexts, in each case involving a pair of transformations, one of which is, or is analogous to, conjugation (either inner automorphism or complex conjugation)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "not-comparable"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "dfaa7d3c-7ac6-3227-893c-d180370b8622",
                        "entry_id": "0ab3f277-1430-37ad-9e1d-0e8fff9e73a8",
                        "glosses": [
                            "That is related to another functor by an adjunction."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "not-comparable"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "a1163b1a-6ae8-3dd5-b495-78fd9a5306e9",
                        "entry_id": "0ab3f277-1430-37ad-9e1d-0e8fff9e73a8",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Having a relationship of the nature of an adjoint (adjoint curve); sharing multiple points with."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "not-comparable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "9d493291-14fc-44f5-bc90-7fd540c76971",
                                "sense_id": "a1163b1a-6ae8-3dd5-b495-78fd9a5306e9",
                                "text": "The sets A + A₀, B + B₀, together, form the complete intersection, with f = 0, of a composite adjoint curve of order m + k, consisting of the adjoint curve of order m through A + B, together with the non-adjoint curve ω = 0; and the set B + B₀ consists of p points, and lies on i + j adjoint φ-curves of f = 0."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ff8c4245-27e9-4fec-ba47-2f4067d07c37",
                                "sense_id": "a1163b1a-6ae8-3dd5-b495-78fd9a5306e9",
                                "text": "As we have stated before, a curve f' is adjoint to a curve f if it have at least the multiplicity r#95;i-1 at each point where f has the multiplicity r#95;i. A first polar #92;sum#95;iy#95;i#92;left(#92;partialf#47;#92;partialx#95;i#92;right)#61;0 is an example of an adjoint curve."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8ece910b-2cb7-4ce6-b0ea-6015a4b7a7d9",
                                "sense_id": "a1163b1a-6ae8-3dd5-b495-78fd9a5306e9",
                                "text": "This imposes n(n - 3)/2 conditions on the n-gon adjoint curve."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
